From ca54cbb5d56449d86242b1fba572e926a1f6881e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:41:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] review release: skip held cards in prepare(),
 restore recompute_ready WHY, fetchone in released(), real-feed resume test

Answers the four non-blocking nits of review t_01a1d29d4b83a3da on PR #1803:

1. prepare() applies recompute_ready's skips before any GitHub read or snapshot:
   a sticky stop or a blocked/triage card at the failure breaker is never promoted
   that tick, so it costs nothing. Both now share kanban_block_routing.recovery_held
   (moved out of kanban_db, behaviour-preserving: the breaker check ran only for
   blocked/triage rows, which have no other side effect before it).
2. recompute_ready's docstring again says why: agent-triage failure-budget recovery,
   the #28712 sticky-block rule and the breaker rationale (kanban_db.py shrinks).
3. released() reads the latest promotion with fetchone() and returns False early
   instead of a LIMIT 1 loop whose continue meant reject.
4. test_kanban_operator_resume_observed_feed: the worker's real dispatcher records
   the active_pr hold, the in-memory Board feed carries it home, the home's own guard
   releases (gh MERGED) and 'hermes kanban resume' accepts the fed observation; the
   worker (gh OPEN) then claims.

Tests: test_held_card_gets_no_external_evaluation (sticky, breaker, task max_retries
breaker/headroom, todo at limit, control); every condition mutation-killed (released() early
return on no promotion: test_kanban_review_release_boundaries[no_promotion]).
